\DOC LOCAL_SQR_P25519_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SQR_P25519_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-state goal past an inlined
modular-squaring subroutine, replacing the current state variable by a
successor state and recording the squaring result as a new named value.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} selecting a variant of the step (one and the
same value throughout the observed traces). The second is a {string list} of
two names: the first is the base name for the fresh variable denoting the
squaring result (a primed form may be used if that name is already taken), and
the second names the existing input value whose square modulo the prime is
taken.
{
   A ?- eventually ... t
   =====================================  LOCAL_SQR_P25519_TAC n ["x1"; "x2"]
   A' ?- eventually ... u
}
\noindent Here the assumptions in {A} describe one state variable, and {u} is a
fresh successor state variable. In {A'}, all state-indexed assumptions
(program-counter value, {bytes_loaded}, register and memory reads, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame relation) are discharged and re-added for {u},
with an updated program-counter offset and with the memory read at the
destination location now giving the fresh result variable; a new purely
arithmetic assumption equating that fresh variable with the square of the named
input reduced modulo the prime is added; the non-state arithmetic and
non-overlapping assumptions of {A} are retained, possibly reordered.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-subgoal state-advancing behaviour,
always on goals of this assembly-verification shape, with the same integer
argument and a two-name list.

\ENDDOC
